# Banking Reports Web Architecture Rules

## 1. Purpose

This repository contains the banking Reports microfrontend.

Its primary responsibility is to provide a desktop web interface for consulting, searching, filtering, sorting, paginating and exporting banking requests.

The main experience is the **Requests Dashboard**.

The reference UI mockups are the visual source of truth.

This microfrontend must focus on reporting and request consultation.

It must not implement:

* customer onboarding flows
* contract generation
* loan orchestration
* product selection flows
* customer registration
* downstream business processes owned by other microfrontends

---

# 2. Primary Experience

The main page is the Requests Dashboard.

Preferred route:

`/requests`

The page must provide:

* requests table
* DNI search
* sortable columns
* request status visualization
* pagination
* total request count
* export action
* loading state
* empty state
* error state

The requests table is the primary visual and functional element of the page.

Do not replace the table with cards unless explicitly required by a future design.

---

# 3. Design Philosophy

The UI must prioritize:

* clean banking interface
* professional enterprise appearance
* pixel-perfect implementation
* desktop-first layout
* information density
* readability
* reusable components
* accessibility
* consistent spacing
* production-ready code

The provided UI mockup is the design source of truth.

Cursor must reproduce the reference design as accurately as possible and must not redesign the screen based on personal assumptions.

---

# 4. Desktop-First Layout

This application primarily targets desktop browsers.

Rules:

* design for desktop first
* minimum supported width: 1280px
* optimize especially for 1366px, 1440px and 1920px screens
* table content should use the available horizontal space efficiently
* avoid unnecessary narrow containers
* responsive behavior is secondary
* do not transform the desktop table into cards unless explicitly requested
* preserve readable column spacing at supported desktop resolutions

The main reports area should feel wide and data-oriented.

---

# 5. Main Layout Structure

Use a consistent application layout.

```text
ReportsLayout

├── Header
│
└── Main Content
    │
    ├── Page Header
    │   ├── Page Title
    │   └── Toolbar
    │       ├── DNI Search
    │       └── Export Action
    │
    └── Requests Table Container
        ├── Table Header
        ├── Table Rows
        └── Table Footer
            ├── Result Summary
            └── Pagination
```

Avoid unnecessary wrappers that do not provide semantic, structural or styling value.

---

# 6. Header

The application header must be an independent reusable component.

Responsibilities may include:

* Santander Consumer logo
* Search navigation
* Requests navigation
* authenticated user information
* user avatar or initials
* logout action

The header must not contain reports-specific business logic.

Preferred component:

`partner-header.component.ts`

or, if the repository is reports-specific:

`reports-header.component.ts`

Reuse an existing shared header if the project already provides one.

Do not duplicate an existing header implementation.

---

# 7. Requests Page

Preferred page component:

`requests-page.component.ts`

The page should orchestrate the feature but remain lightweight.

Responsibilities:

* initialize requests
* coordinate search
* coordinate sorting
* coordinate pagination
* coordinate export
* consume feature state
* compose presentation components

The page must not contain large amounts of table markup or API implementation logic.

---

# 8. Page Header and Toolbar

The page header should reproduce the reference layout.

Expected structure:

```text
Bandeja de solicitudes                         Buscar DNI
                                                [ input       🔍 ]  Exportar
```

The title should remain visually separated from the toolbar.

Preferred reusable components:

* `requests-toolbar.component.ts`
* `dni-search.component.ts`
* `export-action.component.ts`

Do not create separate components for trivial elements unless reuse or complexity justifies them.

---

# 9. DNI Search

The search field is specifically intended to search requests by customer DNI.

Preferred internal naming:

* `dniSearch`
* `searchByDni()`
* `dniSearchControl`

Use Angular Reactive Forms.

Expected behavior:

* accept the required DNI format
* prevent invalid input when appropriate
* support keyboard submission
* allow search through the search icon
* expose loading behavior
* handle no-result scenarios
* preserve accessibility

Do not implement filtering only at presentation level when the real API is expected to perform server-side search.

API integration must remain isolated from the UI component.

---

# 10. Requests Table

The requests table is the core component of this feature.

Preferred component:

`requests-table.component.ts`

The table should reproduce the visual structure shown in the reference mockup.

Expected columns:

* Request Date
* Customer DNI
* Transaction Amount
* Branch
* Request Status

Internal code and models must use English naming even if visible UI labels are displayed in Spanish.

Example:

```ts
export interface RequestItem {
  requestDate: string;
  customerDni: string;
  transactionAmount: number;
  branch: string;
  status: RequestStatus;
}
```

Do not use Spanish property names in TypeScript models.

---

# 11. Table Responsibilities

The table presentation component may be responsible for:

* rendering headers
* rendering rows
* displaying formatted values
* emitting sorting events
* displaying status badges

It should not be responsible for:

* calling APIs
* managing backend pagination
* exporting files
* storing global feature state
* implementing business orchestration

Keep data retrieval outside the table component.

---

# 12. Sorting

Sortable columns must clearly indicate their sorting capability.

The reference design uses sorting indicators beside column labels.

Possible sortable fields:

* request date
* customer DNI
* transaction amount
* branch
* request status

Use an explicit model:

```ts
export type SortDirection = 'asc' | 'desc';

export interface RequestSort {
  field: RequestSortField;
  direction: SortDirection;
}
```

Sorting behavior should be controlled by the feature/page layer.

If sorting is server-side, send the selected sorting configuration through the API service.

Do not duplicate sorting logic across components.

---

# 13. Request Status

Request statuses must use a reusable visual component.

Preferred component:

`request-status-badge.component.ts`

Example status model:

```ts
export type RequestStatus =
  | 'approved'
  | 'pending'
  | 'rejected';
```

Visible labels may be:

* Aprobado
* Pendiente
* Rechazado

The visual treatment should follow the provided mockup.

Expected semantic styles:

* approved → positive/success appearance
* pending → warning appearance
* rejected → negative/rejection appearance

Do not hardcode status presentation independently in every table row.

Centralize status-to-label and status-to-style mapping.

---

# 14. Pagination

Pagination must be implemented as a reusable component.

Preferred component:

`pagination.component.ts`

Expected information:

* current page
* page size
* total records
* total pages

The reference UI includes:

* current result range
* total number of requests
* previous action
* page numbers
* ellipsis where appropriate
* next action

Example:

`1 a 15 de 1,233 solicitudes`

Pagination must support large datasets.

Do not render hundreds of page buttons simultaneously.

---

# 15. Pagination State

Use an explicit model.

```ts
export interface PaginationState {
  page: number;
  pageSize: number;
  totalItems: number;
  totalPages: number;
}
```

Pagination should preferably be server-side when consuming a paginated backend API.

Changing pages should trigger the corresponding data request rather than loading the complete dataset into the browser unless the API contract explicitly requires otherwise.

---

# 16. Export

The page provides an Export action.

Preferred component or action:

`export-action.component.ts`

Preferred service responsibility:

`requests-export.service.ts`

The UI should only trigger the export request.

Export implementation must remain outside the presentation component.

Possible export responsibilities:

* preserve active filters when required
* preserve search criteria when required
* request the export from the appropriate API
* manage export loading state
* handle export errors
* trigger the resulting file download

Do not generate fake CSV or Excel data unless explicitly requested.

Do not assume the export format until defined by the backend contract.

---

# 17. Feature State

The reports feature should maintain centralized reactive state.

Prefer Angular Signals.

Suggested state:

```ts
export interface RequestsState {
  requests: RequestItem[];
  loading: boolean;
  error: string | null;

  dniSearch: string;

  sort: RequestSort | null;

  pagination: PaginationState;
}
```

State should be:

* explicit
* reactive
* predictable
* feature-scoped
* immutable when practical

Avoid mutable global variables.

---

# 18. Derived State

Use `computed()` for derived UI information when appropriate.

Examples:

* has results
* is empty
* current result range
* can navigate previous
* can navigate next

Avoid manually synchronizing values that can be derived from existing state.

---

# 19. API Services

Separate API responsibilities from presentation.

Preferred service:

`requests-api.service.ts`

Possible responsibilities:

* retrieve requests
* search by DNI
* request paginated data
* send sorting parameters

Export logic may remain in:

`requests-export.service.ts`

Do not create generic services such as:

* `api.service.ts`
* `data.service.ts`
* `service.service.ts`

Service names must clearly describe their responsibility.

---

# 20. API Models

Separate API DTOs from UI/domain models when the backend contract differs from the presentation model.

Recommended folders:

```text
models/
├── request-item.model.ts
├── request-status.model.ts
├── request-query.model.ts
└── pagination.model.ts

interfaces/
└── requests-api-response.interface.ts
```

Do not bind raw API responses directly to complex UI logic when transformation is required.

---

# 21. Query Model

Search, sorting and pagination parameters should be represented explicitly.

Example:

```ts
export interface RequestsQuery {
  dni?: string;
  page: number;
  pageSize: number;
  sortField?: RequestSortField;
  sortDirection?: SortDirection;
}
```

This object should represent the current report query.

Avoid spreading query parameters across unrelated variables.

---

# 22. Recommended Feature Architecture

Prefer feature-first organization.

```text
src/app/

├── core/
│   ├── services/
│   ├── interceptors/
│   └── guards/
│
├── shared/
│   ├── components/
│   ├── directives/
│   ├── pipes/
│   └── models/
│
├── layouts/
│   └── reports-layout/
│
└── features/
    └── reports/
        ├── pages/
        │   └── requests-page/
        │
        ├── components/
        │   ├── requests-toolbar/
        │   ├── dni-search/
        │   ├── requests-table/
        │   ├── request-status-badge/
        │   ├── pagination/
        │   ├── empty-state/
        │   └── error-state/
        │
        ├── services/
        │   ├── requests-api.service.ts
        │   └── requests-export.service.ts
        │
        ├── state/
        │   └── requests-state.service.ts
        │
        ├── models/
        │
        ├── interfaces/
        │
        └── utils/
```

Do not create folders that remain empty or have no architectural purpose.

---

# 23. Shared vs Feature Components

Keep reports-specific components inside the reports feature.

Examples:

* requests table
* DNI search
* request status badge
* requests toolbar

Move a component to `shared/` only when it is genuinely reusable by multiple independent features.

Do not prematurely move everything into `shared`.

---

# 24. Routing

Routes must represent the business domain.

Preferred route:

`/requests`

Possible future routes:

`/requests/:id`

`/reports`

Avoid generic routes such as:

* `/home`
* `/table`
* `/page`
* `/list`
* `/screen1`

Use lazy-loaded standalone components where appropriate.

---

# 25. Loading State

Loading must be explicit.

When requests are being retrieved:

* prevent duplicate requests where appropriate
* communicate loading visually
* preserve layout stability
* avoid rendering misleading empty results

Do not display the empty state while the initial request is still loading.

---

# 26. Empty State

If no requests exist for the current query, display an intentional empty state.

Example visible message:

`No se encontraron solicitudes.`

The empty state should remain inside the table/content area whenever possible so the page structure remains stable.

Do not leave the page blank.

---

# 27. Error State

API failures must produce a controlled error state.

The UI must:

* communicate that requests could not be loaded
* avoid exposing raw backend errors
* allow retry when appropriate
* preserve the surrounding application layout

Technical errors may be logged through the project's existing logging mechanism.

---

# 28. Styling

Use SCSS.

Follow the project's established BEM methodology.

Examples:

```scss
.requests-page {}
.requests-page__header {}
.requests-page__toolbar {}
.requests-page__table {}
.requests-page__pagination {}
```

Avoid:

* inline styles
* duplicated declarations
* deeply nested selectors
* unnecessary `!important`
* generic class names
* global styles for feature-specific elements

---

# 29. Design Tokens

Prefer existing Santander or project design tokens.

Use tokens for:

* colors
* typography
* spacing
* borders
* radius
* shadows

Do not repeatedly hardcode equivalent visual values across components.

If a required token already exists in the project, reuse it instead of creating another one.

---

# 30. Visual Accuracy

The provided mockup must be treated as the visual reference.

Pay special attention to:

* Santander header proportions
* horizontal page spacing
* page title position
* DNI search alignment
* Export action alignment
* table width
* column distribution
* row height
* table header typography
* subtle row separators
* rounded table container
* status badge dimensions
* pagination alignment
* total records position
* background color
* whitespace

Do not redesign these elements without explicit instruction.

---

# 31. Table Layout Stability

Avoid visual movement when data changes.

Rules:

* maintain consistent row heights
* maintain stable column widths
* avoid resizing the entire table when status text changes
* align numeric values consistently
* keep pagination position stable
* prevent search loading from shifting the page unnecessarily

---

# 32. Accessibility

Use semantic HTML whenever possible.

Prefer an actual HTML table structure for tabular information.

Use:

* `<table>`
* `<thead>`
* `<tbody>`
* `<th>`
* `<tr>`
* `<td>`

Sorting controls must be keyboard accessible.

Search input must have an associated label.

Buttons must have meaningful accessible names.

Status information must not depend exclusively on color.

Use ARIA only when native HTML semantics are insufficient.

---

# 33. Angular Practices

Use Angular 20 practices.

Prefer:

* standalone components
* Signals
* `computed()`
* `inject()`
* Reactive Forms
* lazy loading
* typed interfaces
* OnPush when appropriate
* modern Angular template control flow

Example:

```html
@if (loading()) {
  ...
} @else if (error()) {
  ...
} @else if (requests().length === 0) {
  ...
} @else {
  ...
}
```

Avoid unnecessary manual subscriptions.

---

# 34. Naming

Code identifiers and filenames must be in English.

Preferred:

* `requests-page.component.ts`
* `requests-table.component.ts`
* `requests-toolbar.component.ts`
* `request-status-badge.component.ts`
* `requests-api.service.ts`
* `requests-state.service.ts`
* `requests-export.service.ts`
* `pagination.component.ts`

Visible UI text may remain in Spanish according to the design.

Do not translate business-facing labels unless explicitly requested.

---

# 35. Separation of Responsibilities

Maintain clear boundaries.

Page:

* orchestrates the feature

State:

* manages reactive feature state

API service:

* communicates with backend

Table:

* presents requests

Search:

* captures DNI criteria

Pagination:

* emits page changes

Status badge:

* presents request status

Export service:

* handles export integration

Do not mix these responsibilities unnecessarily.

---

# 36. Data Flow

Prefer predictable one-directional data flow.

```text
User Action
    ↓
Page / Feature
    ↓
State or Service
    ↓
API
    ↓
Response
    ↓
State
    ↓
UI
```

Child presentation components should preferably receive data through inputs and communicate actions through outputs.

Avoid child components directly modifying unrelated application state.

---

# 37. Testing Considerations

Components and services should be easy to test independently.

Prioritize tests for:

* DNI search behavior
* sorting events
* pagination
* status mapping
* empty state
* error state
* API query construction
* export triggering

Avoid architecture that requires rendering the entire application to test basic feature behavior.

---

# 38. Performance

Prefer:

* server-side pagination
* server-side filtering when supported
* server-side sorting when supported
* Signals
* OnPush
* immutable updates
* `track` expressions in repeated templates

Avoid:

* loading the entire request history unnecessarily
* repeated API calls for unchanged queries
* unnecessary subscriptions
* expensive template calculations

---

# 39. Cursor Responsibilities

When generating code for this repository, Cursor should:

* preserve the reports domain
* use the provided mockup as visual source of truth
* prioritize the requests dashboard
* preserve desktop-first behavior
* generate modular Angular 20 code
* use standalone components
* use Signals where appropriate
* use Reactive Forms for DNI search
* separate table presentation from API communication
* implement reusable status badges
* implement scalable pagination
* keep search, sorting and pagination state explicit
* preserve feature-first architecture
* reuse existing project components and design tokens whenever available
* keep code identifiers in English
* keep visible UI labels aligned with the provided Spanish design
* generate production-quality code

---

# 40. Cursor Must Avoid

Do not:

* redesign the supplied mockup
* convert the requests table into cards
* create giant page components
* put API calls inside table components
* mix export logic with presentation logic
* hardcode fake backend responses into production components
* duplicate status styling
* duplicate pagination logic
* create unnecessary global state
* use mutable global variables
* create generic service names
* create generic routes
* use inline CSS
* create unnecessary wrappers
* introduce mobile-first behavior that compromises the desktop design
* invent request fields not present in the requirements
* invent backend endpoints
* invent export formats
* invent business rules

---

# 41. Final Architecture Principle

The Reports microfrontend must remain:

* data-oriented
* modular
* desktop-first
* scalable
* accessible
* predictable
* visually consistent
* isolated from unrelated banking flows

The **Requests Dashboard** is the primary experience.

The requests table, DNI search, sorting, status representation, pagination and export capabilities must be implemented as clear and maintainable responsibilities rather than as one monolithic page.
